_DATA = json.loads("{\"why-llm-apps-fail\": {\"situation\": \"Your team shipped an email assistant after a great demo. A month later, support tickets say it's \\\"unreliable\\\": wrong senders, odd summaries, inconsistent formats. Your manager suggests buying an evaluation tool with a hallucination score. The interviewer asks:\", \"q\": \"Your email assistant worked in the demo, and real users say it's unreliable. Where do you start?\", \"analogy\": \"Think of a new assistant who keeps getting your errands wrong. Sometimes they never saw the shop (they didn't know the data). Sometimes your note just said \\\"get milk\\\" (you didn't say which). And sometimes the note was clear and they still forgot. Only the last one needs you to start keeping score.\", \"cards\": [\"Three gulfs. Know your data. Say what you want. Then measure what still fails.\", [[\"Comprehension\", [\"Read real\", \"traces\"], \"Look first\", \"a\"], [\"Specification\", [\"Edit the\", \"prompt\"], \"Cheap fix\", \"info\"], [\"Generalization\", [\"Measure with\", \"an eval\"], \"Never zero\", \"bad\"]]], \"caption\": \"Evals are for what survives a clear prompt.\", \"try\": \"<b>Try to break it.</b> Pick each failure and diagnose it. Which ones need an eval?\", \"why\": \"<b>Now say why:</b> why build an eval for only one of the four?\", \"how\": [\"c2\"], \"checklist\": [\"Read real traces before writing metrics.\", \"Comprehension: you haven't looked at the data.\", \"Specification: the prompt doesn't say what you want.\", \"Generalization: clear prompt, model still slips.\", \"Fix specification with a prompt edit.\", \"Build evals for generalization failures.\", \"Analyze, measure, improve, repeat.\"], \"trap_title\": \"\\\"Let's buy a tool with a hallucination score\\\"\", \"trap_html\": \"<p>It sounds efficient, but a generic score doesn't know your app's failures, and many of them are specification gaps a prompt edit would fix. Read your traces first, then build checks for the failures that remain.</p>\"}, \"choosing-the-right-eval\": {\"situation\": \"Your team built an LLM judge that checks answers for invented policy details. It agrees with experts 93% of the time. The product manager wants it on every answer before users see it; engineering wants it in CI only. The interviewer asks:\", \"q\": \"Should this check run in CI, on live traffic, or before every answer reaches the user?\", \"analogy\": \"Think of quality checks in a bakery. A metal detector checks every loaf on the line in a split second. A taster samples a few loaves each hour. A new recipe gets a full panel before launch. Each check does a different job, and none could do the others'.\", \"cards\": [\"Four places a check can live. Every answer, inline. CI, before release. A sample of live traffic. A human queue.\", [[\"Guardrail\", [\"Every answer,\", \"milliseconds\"], \"Mostly code\", \"bad\"], [\"CI\", [\"Fixed cases,\", \"every change\"], \"Near 100%\", \"ok\"], [\"Monitor\", [\"1–5% sample,\", \"async\"], \"Judges fit\", \"info\"]]], \"caption\": \"Latency and cost decide where a check lives.\", \"try\": \"<b>Try to break it.</b> Put the LLM judge inline with a 200 ms budget. Then raise traffic.\", \"why\": \"<b>Now say why:</b> why does sampling make the judge affordable without losing the failure rate?\", \"how\": [\"c2\"], \"checklist\": [\"Give every eval a job.\", \"Capability evals start low.\", \"Regression evals pass near 100%.\", \"Monitors sample live traffic async.\", \"Guardrails run inline and must be fast.\", \"LLM judges take seconds and cost money.\", \"Humans find new failures and label data.\"], \"trap_title\": \"\\\"Run the judge on every answer to be safe\\\"\", \"trap_html\": \"<p>It sounds safer, but it adds seconds to every reply and costs far more, and a 2% sample gives the same failure rate. Keep inline checks fast and cheap, and run judges after the fact.</p>\"}, \"building-an-eval-dataset\": {\"situation\": \"You're about to launch a real estate assistant with no users yet. A teammate generated 200 test queries with one prompt, and the app passes 96% of them. The interviewer asks:\", \"q\": \"You have no users yet. How do you build a test set that finds real failures?\", \"analogy\": \"Think of testing a new car by driving it round the same sunny block 200 times. It passes every lap, and you've learnt nothing about rain, gravel or a steep hill. Pick the conditions first, then drive each one.\", \"cards\": [\"Three steps for synthetic data. Pick dimensions. Combine them into tuples. Turn tuples into queries.\", [[\"Dimensions\", [\"Where it\", \"might fail\"], \"Feature, user, scenario\", \"a\"], [\"Tuples\", [\"One value\", \"from each\"], \"20 by hand\", \"info\"], [\"Queries\", [\"Separate\", \"LLM step\"], \"Filter hard\", \"ok\"]]], \"caption\": \"A test set can only find the failures it contains.\", \"try\": \"<b>Try to break it.</b> Add scenarios and features. Then ask for more queries the direct way.\", \"why\": \"<b>Now say why:</b> why does asking for more queries not fix a direct prompt?\", \"how\": [\"c2\", \"c3\"], \"checklist\": [\"Start with about 100 traces.\", \"Prefer production logs, plus bad experiences.\", \"Don't ask an LLM for queries directly.\", \"Define dimensions where the app may fail.\", \"Write tuples, then turn them into queries.\", \"Stop at saturation.\", \"Synthetic sets find failures; real traffic measures them.\"], \"trap_title\": \"\\\"It passes 96% of 200 test queries\\\"\", \"trap_html\": \"<p>It sounds reassuring, but if one prompt wrote those queries, they're mostly the same easy request. Check which combinations of feature, user and scenario they cover before trusting the number.</p>\"}, \"error-analysis\": {\"situation\": \"Your real estate assistant has 100 logged traces. A teammate pasted them into an LLM and asked for the top failure categories. It returned \\\"hallucination, tone, instruction following\\\", and the team wants to build a judge for each. The interviewer asks:\", \"q\": \"You have 100 traces. How do you turn reading them into numbers you can act on?\", \"analogy\": \"Think of a doctor learning a new disease. They don't start from a textbook's list of symptoms; they examine patients and write down what they see, then group the notes. The textbook's categories help when stuck, but the patients decide.\", \"cards\": [\"Four steps. Note the first failure. Group the notes. Label every trace. Count.\", [[\"Open code\", [\"First failure,\", \"in your words\"], \"You do it\", \"a\"], [\"Axial code\", [\"Group into\", \"app-specific modes\"], \"LLM can help\", \"info\"], [\"Count\", [\"1 or 0\", \"per mode\"], \"Rates to rank\", \"ok\"]]], \"caption\": \"Your traces decide the categories, not a generic list.\", \"try\": \"<b>Try to break it.</b> Read only 20 traces. Then 300. Watch the rare modes.\", \"why\": \"<b>Now say why:</b> why does reading more stop helping, yet rare modes still hide?\", \"how\": [\"c2\", \"c3\"], \"checklist\": [\"Read about 100 traces as the user would.\", \"Note the first failure in each.\", \"Give each trace a pass or fail.\", \"Group notes into app-specific modes.\", \"Split modes that need different fixes.\", \"Label 1 or 0 and compute rates.\", \"Stop at saturation, repeat after changes.\"], \"trap_title\": \"\\\"An LLM gave us the failure categories\\\"\", \"trap_html\": \"<p>It sounds fast, but generic categories like \\\"hallucination\\\" don't match your app's real failures, and checks built on them measure the wrong things. Read the traces yourself first; let an LLM help only with grouping your notes.</p>\"}, \"reviewing-traces-at-scale\": {\"situation\": \"Your assistant serves 50,000 conversations a day. The team reviews 50 a week, all taken from thumbs-down feedback. Quality looks stable, yet a big customer reports a problem nobody has seen. The interviewer asks:\", \"q\": \"You have 50,000 traces a day and time to read 50. Which 50?\", \"analogy\": \"Think of a fishing boat that only checks the nets where the sonar beeped. It catches what it knows about, and never learns there are fish in the quiet water. Cast some nets at random too.\", \"cards\": [\"Three ways to pick traces. Random for honesty. Flagged for known problems. Disagreements for borderline cases.\", [[\"Random\", [\"Unbiased\", \"picture\"], \"Mostly passes\", \"info\"], [\"Flagged\", [\"Thumbs-down,\", \"guardrails\"], \"Known only\", \"bad\"], [\"Uncertain\", [\"Checks\", \"disagree\"], \"Sharpens rubric\", \"ok\"]]], \"caption\": \"You only find the failures your sampling can reach.\", \"try\": \"<b>Try to break it.</b> Spend all 50 reviews on flagged traces. Then try the mix.\", \"why\": \"<b>Now say why:</b> why does failure-driven sampling never find a new problem?\", \"how\": [\"c2\"], \"checklist\": [\"You can't read everything, so choose.\", \"Random sampling is honest but slow.\", \"Failure-driven sampling misses unflagged problems.\", \"Uncertainty sampling finds borderline cases.\", \"Read some traces that passed everything.\", \"Cluster, plot and search to see patterns.\", \"Build a small review tool.\"], \"trap_title\": \"\\\"We review every thumbs-down, so we'd know\\\"\", \"trap_html\": \"<p>It sounds thorough, but most unhappy users never click thumbs-down, and some failures don't look bad to the user at all. Add random and disagreement-based samples, and read traces that passed every check.</p>\"}, \"code-based-evaluators\": {\"situation\": \"Your data agent answers numeric questions. Its pass rate on a 200-question eval is 41%, far below what users report. A teammate wants to switch models. The interviewer asks:\", \"q\": \"Before reaching for an LLM judge, what can you check with plain code, and how can code checks go wrong?\", \"analogy\": \"Think of a teacher marking a maths test with an answer key that says 0.50. A strict marker fails every student who wrote 0.5 or ½; a careless one accepts 0.55. Test the marking on a few papers you've already checked before marking the whole class.\", \"cards\": [\"Three properties of a good code check. Fast. Repeatable. Graded fairly.\", [[\"Objective rules\", [\"Schema, SQL,\", \"tool names\"], \"Code, not judges\", \"a\"], [\"Execute\", [\"Run SQL\", \"and tests\"], \"Compare results\", \"info\"], [\"Test it\", [\"Known right\", \"and wrong\"], \"Fair grading\", \"ok\"]]], \"caption\": \"A grading bug looks exactly like a weak model.\", \"try\": \"<b>Try to break it.</b> Switch between the three graders.\", \"why\": \"<b>Now say why:</b> why does rounding to the task's precision beat both other graders?\", \"how\": [\"c2\"], \"checklist\": [\"Use code for objective failure modes.\", \"Validate format, schema and tool names.\", \"Execute SQL and code; compare results.\", \"Normalise before comparing.\", \"Too strict fails right answers.\", \"Too loose passes wrong ones.\", \"Test the grader on known answers.\"], \"trap_title\": \"\\\"The model only scores 41%, so let's switch models\\\"\", \"trap_html\": \"<p>It sounds decisive, but a strict grader can make a good model look bad. Read some failures first: if right answers are failing, fix the grader, not the model.</p>\"}, \"validating-an-llm-judge\": {\"situation\": \"Your team's tone judge reports that 88% of client emails pass. A week of complaints says tone is often wrong. The judge was checked by eyeballing ten examples. The interviewer asks:\", \"q\": \"Your LLM judge says 85% of answers pass. How do you know the real pass rate?\", \"analogy\": \"Think of a medical screening test. If it misses some sick people and flags some healthy ones, the share of positives isn't the share of sick people. Knowing the test's two error rates lets you work back to the real number.\", \"cards\": [\"Three steps. Split the labels. Measure both error rates. Correct the raw number.\", [[\"Split\", [\"Train, dev,\", \"test once\"], \"No leakage\", \"a\"], [\"Measure\", [\"TPR and TNR\", \"separately\"], \"~90% each\", \"info\"], [\"Correct\", [\"Rogan–Gladen,\", \"bootstrap CI\"], \"Honest rate\", \"ok\"]]], \"caption\": \"A judge's raw number is biased until you correct it.\", \"try\": \"<b>Try to break it.</b> Lower the judge's TNR. Then push both rates towards 50%.\", \"why\": \"<b>Now say why:</b> why does a low TNR make the app look better than it is?\", \"how\": [\"c2\", \"c3\"], \"checklist\": [\"Treat the judge as a classifier.\", \"Split labels into train, dev and test.\", \"Never reuse prompt examples in dev or test.\", \"Measure TPR and TNR separately.\", \"Correct the raw pass rate.\", \"Bootstrap for a confidence interval.\", \"Pin the model and re-check weekly.\"], \"trap_title\": \"\\\"The judge looked right on ten examples\\\"\", \"trap_html\": \"<p>It sounds like enough, but ten examples can't measure how often the judge misses failures, and a judge with a low TNR reports a rosy pass rate. Measure TPR and TNR on a held-out test set and correct the number.</p>\"}, \"labelling-with-a-team\": {\"situation\": \"Three people labelled 50 support answers for tone and agree 86% of the time. You're about to use their labels to validate a tone judge. Most answers are fine. The interviewer asks:\", \"q\": \"Two experts label the same 50 answers and agree 90% of the time. Is your rubric good?\", \"analogy\": \"Think of two weather forecasters in a desert. If both say \\\"sunny\\\" every day, they agree 95% of the time and you've learnt nothing about their skill. What matters is whether they agree on the rare rainy days.\", \"cards\": [\"Three steps. Label independently. Measure kappa per criterion. Align on disagreements.\", [[\"Independent\", [\"Same 20–50\", \"traces\"], \"No peeking\", \"a\"], [\"Kappa\", [\"Beyond\", \"chance\"], \"Per criterion\", \"info\"], [\"Align\", [\"Sharpen the\", \"rubric\"], \"Relabel\", \"ok\"]]], \"caption\": \"Agreement on easy cases is free; agreement on failures is what counts.\", \"try\": \"<b>Try to break it.</b> Keep disagreements fixed and move labels from both-pass to both-fail.\", \"why\": \"<b>Now say why:</b> why does the same 84% agreement give such different kappas?\", \"how\": [\"c2\", \"c3\"], \"checklist\": [\"Let one trusted expert decide when possible.\", \"Draft a rubric per criterion with examples.\", \"Label 20–50 shared traces independently.\", \"Use kappa, not percent agreement.\", \"Measure each criterion separately.\", \"Turn disagreements into rubric changes.\", \"Save labels, decisions and edge cases.\"], \"trap_title\": \"\\\"Our annotators agree 86% of the time\\\"\", \"trap_html\": \"<p>It sounds solid, but if most answers pass, much of that agreement is chance. Compute kappa per criterion, and look at whether they agree on the failures.</p>\"}, \"multi-turn-evals\": {\"situation\": \"Your shopping assistant scores 92% on a single-question test set. In production, long conversations end with users repeating themselves, and some leave with the wrong product. The interviewer asks:\", \"q\": \"Your chatbot answers single questions well and falls apart in long conversations. How do you evaluate that?\", \"analogy\": \"Think of a waiter taking a big table's order one person at a time. If they start cooking after the first person speaks, later changes get lost. Judge whether the table got their meals, then find the moment the order went wrong.\", \"cards\": [\"Three levels. Did the session succeed? Which turn failed? Did it remember?\", [[\"Session\", [\"User's goal\", \"met?\"], \"Start here\", \"a\"], [\"Turn\", [\"First bad\", \"reply\"], \"Failures only\", \"info\"], [\"Memory\", [\"Earlier facts\", \"kept?\"], \"Coherence\", \"ok\"]]], \"caption\": \"Models lose reliability over turns more than ability.\", \"try\": \"<b>Try to break it.</b> Switch from all at once to several turns.\", \"why\": \"<b>Now say why:</b> why do the worst runs fall so much further than the best?\", \"how\": [\"c2\"], \"checklist\": [\"One pass or fail per conversation first.\", \"Diagnose failed sessions turn by turn.\", \"Check memory of earlier facts.\", \"Reproduce failures as single-turn tests.\", \"Simulate users with hidden goals.\", \"Report success by conversation length.\", \"Perturb passing conversations.\"], \"trap_title\": \"\\\"It scores 92% on our question set, so chat works\\\"\", \"trap_html\": \"<p>It sounds like good coverage, but single questions give the model everything at once. Real users reveal needs over several turns, where models get much less reliable. Evaluate whole conversations.</p>\"}, \"simulated-users-and-environments\": {\"situation\": \"Your telecom support agent can change account settings, while customers must restart phones and toggle settings themselves. It passed a test set of scripted conversations, yet real customers get stuck halfway through fixes. The interviewer asks:\", \"q\": \"Your support agent changes databases and talks to customers. How do you test it before real customers do?\", \"analogy\": \"Think of a doctor giving first-aid instructions over the phone. Knowing the right treatment is half of it; the other half is explaining clearly, checking what the caller actually did, and fixing their mistakes. Test both halves.\", \"cards\": [\"Three parts. A sandbox that changes. A user who answers and acts. Checks on the end state.\", [[\"Sandbox\", [\"Tool copies,\", \"reset each run\"], \"Real state\", \"a\"], [\"Sim user\", [\"Hidden goal,\", \"own tools\"], \"Audit it\", \"info\"], [\"End state\", [\"Checked\", \"with code\"], \"Not the reply\", \"ok\"]]], \"caption\": \"Agents fail most when the user has to act too.\", \"try\": \"<b>Try to break it.</b> Switch between agent alone and dual control, for both models.\", \"why\": \"<b>Now say why:</b> why does the same model lose points when the user acts?\", \"how\": [\"c2\"], \"checklist\": [\"Sandbox tools and data, reset each run.\", \"Grade the end state with code.\", \"Simulate users with hidden goals.\", \"Give simulated users tools if real ones act.\", \"Run a no-user ablation.\", \"Audit the simulator's mistakes.\", \"Report pass^k and cost per task.\"], \"trap_title\": \"\\\"It passes our scripted conversations\\\"\", \"trap_html\": \"<p>It sounds tested, but a script can't react when the agent asks something unexpected, and scripted users never make mistakes. Use a simulated user who answers, acts and sometimes gets it wrong.</p>\"}, \"coding-agent-evals\": {\"situation\": \"Your internal coding agent's pass rate on 200 backlog tasks jumped from 61% to 88% after a model upgrade. A reviewer notices one patch changed an assertion in a test file. The interviewer asks:\", \"q\": \"Your coding agent's tests all pass. How do you know it fixed the bug rather than the tests?\", \"analogy\": \"Think of a student who can edit the answer key. A perfect score then tells you nothing. Lock the key away, check whether it was touched, and let the student say \\\"this question is wrong\\\".\", \"cards\": [\"Three defences. Protect the tests. Check the diff. Offer an honest exit.\", [[\"Protect\", [\"Hidden or\", \"read-only tests\"], \"Can't edit grader\", \"a\"], [\"Diff check\", [\"Flag test and\", \"fixture edits\"], \"Plus monitors\", \"info\"], [\"Exit\", [\"Abort and\", \"flag\"], \"54% → 9%\", \"ok\"]]], \"caption\": \"If the agent can edit the grader, the score can lie.\", \"try\": \"<b>Try to break it.</b> Turn the abort option off and on for both models.\", \"why\": \"<b>Now say why:</b> why does an honest way out cut cheating so much?\", \"how\": [\"c2\"], \"checklist\": [\"Grade patches by running tests.\", \"Agents can game tests they can edit.\", \"Keep tests hidden or read-only.\", \"Flag diffs that touch tests or fixtures.\", \"Give the agent an abort option.\", \"Use LLM monitors as a backstop.\", \"Report cost and time per task.\"], \"trap_title\": \"\\\"Pass rate jumped 27 points after the upgrade\\\"\", \"trap_html\": \"<p>It sounds like a better model, but a big jump can also mean the agent learnt to satisfy the tests. Check how many diffs touched tests, and spot-check passing patches before celebrating.</p>\"}, \"multi-agent-evals\": {\"situation\": \"Your research assistant uses a planner, three researcher agents and a reviewer. Reports are often confidently wrong, and each team owning an agent says their agent works fine on its own tests. The interviewer asks:\", \"q\": \"Your planner, researcher and writer agents produce a bad report. Which agent failed, and how do you measure it?\", \"analogy\": \"Think of a relay race where the team keeps losing. Timing each runner alone shows they're all fast. The race is lost in the baton passes, and in the last runner nobody checks for a dropped baton.\", \"cards\": [\"Three failure groups. Bad design. Agents out of sync. Weak checking.\", [[\"Design\", [\"Roles, steps,\", \"stopping\"], \"Spec and loops\", \"a\"], [\"Misalignment\", [\"Lost or\", \"ignored info\"], \"Score hand-offs\", \"bad\"], [\"Verification\", [\"Stops early,\", \"checks wrong\"], \"Test the checker\", \"info\"]]], \"caption\": \"Multi-agent failures hide in the hand-offs.\", \"try\": \"<b>Try to break it.</b> Label each trace, and read the fix it points to.\", \"why\": \"<b>Now say why:</b> why does each group need a different fix?\", \"how\": [\"c2\"], \"checklist\": [\"Grade the end result first.\", \"Label failures with a taxonomy.\", \"Design, misalignment, verification.\", \"Score each hand-off separately.\", \"Test the verifier on known-bad outputs.\", \"Watch loops and premature stopping.\", \"Validate LLM labellers before scaling.\"], \"trap_title\": \"\\\"Every agent passes its own tests\\\"\", \"trap_html\": \"<p>It sounds like the system must work, but most multi-agent failures happen between agents: lost information, ignored inputs and checks that don't check. Evaluate the hand-offs and the verifier, not just each agent.</p>\"}, \"evaluating-mcp-servers\": {\"situation\": \"Your company publishes an MCP server for its CRM. Developers report that agents call contacts_search when they mean deals_search, and sometimes call a different vendor's search tool instead of yours. The interviewer asks:\", \"q\": \"You're shipping an MCP server for your product. How do you test that agents can actually use it?\", \"analogy\": \"Think of labelling switches in a building's control room. With five switches, \\\"lights\\\" is enough. With two hundred from different contractors, you need \\\"floor 3, east, lights\\\", or people flip the wrong one.\", \"cards\": [\"Two layers. Rule checks on every call. Outcome checks on real tasks.\", [[\"Rules\", [\"Name, schema,\", \"execution\"], \"~98% solved\", \"ok\"], [\"Choice\", [\"Right tool among\", \"distractors\"], \"Hard part\", \"bad\"], [\"Outcome\", [\"State checked\", \"by code\"], \"Not the claim\", \"a\"]]], \"caption\": \"Format is mostly solved; choosing and planning aren't.\", \"try\": \"<b>Try to break it.</b> Add tools with vague descriptions. Then make them clear.\", \"why\": \"<b>Now say why:</b> why do clear, namespaced names hold up as servers pile up?\", \"how\": [\"c2\"], \"checklist\": [\"Tool descriptions act as prompts.\", \"Rule-check every call: name, schema, success.\", \"Write tasks that don't name the tools.\", \"Connect distractor servers.\", \"Include tasks that shouldn't call your tool.\", \"Verify outcomes by executing checks.\", \"Test across models and runs.\"], \"trap_title\": \"\\\"Schema compliance is 99%, so the server works\\\"\", \"trap_html\": \"<p>It sounds healthy, but valid calls to the wrong tool are still failures. Benchmarks show models format calls well and choose and plan poorly. Measure outcomes on real tasks with distractors.</p>\"}, \"improving-tools-with-evals\": {\"situation\": \"Over two weeks, your team rewrote its MCP tools' descriptions 15 times, and the eval score climbed from 62% to 85%. Customers say agents still struggle with the same tasks. The interviewer asks:\", \"q\": \"You keep rewriting tool descriptions and the score keeps rising. How do you know you're not overfitting?\", \"analogy\": \"Think of a student who memorises last year's exam. Their practice score soars, and the real exam shows nothing changed. Keep some questions back that you never practise on, and judge progress only by those.\", \"cards\": [\"Three rules. Realistic tasks. A held-out set. Metrics beyond accuracy.\", [[\"Tasks\", [\"Multi-step,\", \"verifiable\"], \"Like real work\", \"a\"], [\"Held out\", [\"Never tuned\", \"on\"], \"Keep or revert\", \"ok\"], [\"Metrics\", [\"Calls, tokens,\", \"errors\"], \"Not just accuracy\", \"info\"]]], \"caption\": \"If only the tuning score rises, you've taught the tools your test.\", \"try\": \"<b>Try to break it.</b> Try each change, and compare the two sets.\", \"why\": \"<b>Now say why:</b> why does the biggest tuning gain get reverted?\", \"how\": [\"c2\"], \"checklist\": [\"Write realistic multi-step tasks.\", \"Give each a verifiable outcome.\", \"Split tuning and held-out tasks.\", \"Collect calls, tokens, runtime and errors.\", \"Read transcripts, including omissions.\", \"Change one thing at a time.\", \"Keep changes only if held-out improves.\"], \"trap_title\": \"\\\"Our tool eval went from 62% to 85%\\\"\", \"trap_html\": \"<p>It sounds like progress, but if you tuned against the same tasks you scored, the gain may not transfer. Check a held-out set, and revert changes that only help the tuning tasks.</p>\"}, \"evals-in-ci\": {\"situation\": \"Your team changed the system prompt to shorten answers. The golden set's pass rate went from 94% to 90% on 150 cases. One engineer says it's noise; another wants to block. The interviewer asks:\", \"q\": \"A prompt change drops your golden-set pass rate from 94% to 90%. Do you block the release?\", \"analogy\": \"Think of a building's fire drill. Passing it means the known exits still work, not that the building is safe from every fire. You still need smoke detectors for the fires nobody planned.\", \"cards\": [\"Three rules. Curate the set. Gate every change. Read what flipped.\", [[\"Curate\", [\"Features, bugs,\", \"edge cases\"], \"100–200 cases\", \"a\"], [\"Gate\", [\"Every prompt,\", \"model, tool\"], \"Pinned versions\", \"info\"], [\"Read flips\", [\"Critical cases\", \"must pass\"], \"Then the average\", \"ok\"]]], \"caption\": \"CI protects known cases; it doesn't measure production.\", \"try\": \"<b>Try to break it.</b> Keep the 4-point drop and grow the golden set.\", \"why\": \"<b>Now say why:</b> why does the same drop mean more on a bigger set?\", \"how\": [\"c2\", \"c3\"], \"checklist\": [\"Build a 100–200 case golden set.\", \"Include past failures as regression tests.\", \"Run it on every change.\", \"Pin app and judge model versions.\", \"Read which cases flipped.\", \"Passing CI isn't production quality.\", \"Treat model upgrades as code changes.\"], \"trap_title\": \"\\\"CI passes, so quality is fine\\\"\", \"trap_html\": \"<p>It sounds like a green light, but CI covers only the cases you chose. New kinds of queries and failures show up only in production. Pair CI with sampled monitoring.</p>\"}, \"online-evals-and-guardrails\": {\"situation\": \"Your support assistant handles 100,000 chats a day. The team runs a hallucination judge on every chat before sending it, which adds three seconds and costs thousands a month, and quality still dipped last week without an alert. The interviewer asks:\", \"q\": \"Your assistant handles 100,000 conversations a day. How do you know today's quality without reading them?\", \"analogy\": \"Think of a water company. It doesn't drink every glass in the city; it samples taps across neighbourhoods every day and tests them in a lab. The filter at the plant handles a few known dangers instantly.\", \"cards\": [\"Three signals. Sampled judges. Inline guardrails. User behaviour.\", [[\"Sample\", [\"1–5% of\", \"traffic, async\"], \"Corrected rates\", \"a\"], [\"Guardrails\", [\"Fast code,\", \"inline\"], \"Safe fallback\", \"bad\"], [\"Users\", [\"Retries,\", \"abandonment\"], \"Early warning\", \"info\"]]], \"caption\": \"A small sample measures quality; the budget goes to slicing.\", \"try\": \"<b>Try to break it.</b> Sample 0.1%, then 10%. Watch cost and the error bar.\", \"why\": \"<b>Now say why:</b> why does a bigger sample buy so little precision?\", \"how\": [\"c2\"], \"checklist\": [\"Log full traces.\", \"Judge a 1–5% sample async.\", \"Correct rates and alert on intervals.\", \"Keep guardrails fast and reliable.\", \"Watch implicit user signals.\", \"A/B test big changes.\", \"Feed failures back into CI.\"], \"trap_title\": \"\\\"Run the hallucination judge on every chat before sending\\\"\", \"trap_html\": \"<p>It sounds thorough, but it adds seconds to every reply and costs a fortune, while a small async sample measures the same rate. Keep inline checks to fast code, and judge after the fact.</p>\"}, \"safety-and-red-team-evals\": {\"situation\": \"Your email agent can read the inbox, search files and send messages. Security asks for proof that a malicious email can't make it forward confidential files. The team has a list of 20 jailbreak prompts and nothing else. The interviewer asks:\", \"q\": \"How do you test that your agent can't be talked into leaking data or misusing its tools?\", \"analogy\": \"Think of testing a bank's security. You don't only try the front door; you post forged letters, call pretending to be the manager, and check whether staff still serve real customers after the new rules. Then you add every trick a real thief tried to the next drill.\", \"cards\": [\"Three rules. Plant attacks where they arrive. Score attacks and usefulness. Re-run on every change.\", [[\"Plant\", [\"Emails, pages,\", \"tool outputs\"], \"Indirect too\", \"a\"], [\"Score both\", [\"Attack success\", \"and utility\"], \"Trade-offs\", \"bad\"], [\"Regress\", [\"Every real\", \"attack kept\"], \"Each change\", \"ok\"]]], \"caption\": \"A defence that blocks everything looks perfect without a utility score.\", \"try\": \"<b>Try to break it.</b> Try each defence. Compare attack success with normal tasks solved.\", \"why\": \"<b>Now say why:</b> why does the injection detector look safest and still lose?\", \"how\": [\"c2\"], \"checklist\": [\"Cover jailbreaks and both kinds of injection.\", \"Plant attacks in emails, pages and tool results.\", \"Test poisoned MCP tool descriptions.\", \"Check outcomes on state with code.\", \"Report attack success and utility together.\", \"Run the suite on every change.\", \"Add real attacks as permanent tests.\"], \"trap_title\": \"\\\"We tested 20 jailbreak prompts and it refused them all\\\"\", \"trap_html\": \"<p>It sounds secure, but agents are mostly attacked indirectly, through content they read while doing a normal task, and through tool descriptions. Plant attacks in that content, and score usefulness as well as refusals.</p>\"}, \"improving-with-evals\": {\"situation\": \"Your agent's evals show a 12% failure rate, mostly missed constraints in multi-step requests, and the monthly bill doubled. One engineer proposes fine-tuning; another wants to switch to the cheapest model. The interviewer asks:\", \"q\": \"Your evals show the agent fails 12% of the time and costs too much. What do you change first?\", \"analogy\": \"Think of fixing a slow, error-prone kitchen. First rewrite the confusing recipe cards. Then reorganise the stations. Hiring a new head chef comes last. For cost, let a junior cook handle simple orders and call the chef only when unsure.\", \"cards\": [\"Three moves. Fix cheapest first. Cut tokens and models. Cascade the rest.\", [[\"Fix\", [\"Prompt, then\", \"structure\"], \"Fine-tune last\", \"a\"], [\"Trim\", [\"Smaller models,\", \"caching\"], \"Check evals\", \"info\"], [\"Cascade\", [\"Cheap first,\", \"oracle if unsure\"], \"Target agreement\", \"ok\"]]], \"caption\": \"Evals decide which change stays.\", \"try\": \"<b>Try to break it.</b> Let the cheap model answer everything. Then lower its agreement.\", \"why\": \"<b>Now say why:</b> why does the threshold trade cost against agreement?\", \"how\": [\"c2\"], \"checklist\": [\"Prompt edits for specification failures.\", \"Structural changes for multi-step errors.\", \"Prompt optimisation with 50+ labels.\", \"Fine-tune last, for narrow tasks.\", \"Right-size models and trim tokens.\", \"Make prompts cache-friendly.\", \"Cascade with a held-out threshold.\"], \"trap_title\": \"\\\"Let's fine-tune to fix the 12% failures\\\"\", \"trap_html\": \"<p>It sounds powerful, but if the failures are missed constraints, a clearer prompt or splitting the task may fix them in a day. Fine-tuning needs labelled data and maintenance; try the cheaper fixes first and let the evals decide.</p>\"}}")

